    "Mercury-3":  "The part of you that wants to talk it through runs strong in the daily small moves of love. Communication is one of your steady relational tools. Send the brief notes; have the quick conversations. Small messages build the bonds that hold the bigger work later.",
    "Mercury-4":  "The part of you that wants to talk it through is anchored in home and inner ground. The bond keeps connecting articulation to roots — the why you love started somewhere old. Write a paragraph about it sometime; the patterns get clearer when seen on paper.",
    "Mercury-5":  "The part of you that wants to talk it through is anchored in romance. The bond keeps connecting words to play — flirtation, naming, articulating what you make of love. Do the work of putting language on the playful pieces; naming the love makes it real.",
    "Mercury-6":  "The part of you that wants to talk it through is anchored in the daily-tending. Articulation in love shows up in small precise communication — careful texts, clear scheduling, tidy small messages. Be tidy in the words; clean small communication is one of the underrated crafts of staying close.",
    "Mercury-7":  "The part of you that wants to talk it through is anchored in your closest bond. Conversation with the partner is one of the relationship's main engines. Have the talk. Most close bonds flourish or wither based on how the conversations go.",
    "Mercury-8":  "The part of you that wants to talk it through is anchored in shared depths. Articulation here is harder — hidden things, power dynamics, truths you are still learning to name. Take notes for yourself first; some conversations are not ready until written privately.",
    "Mercury-9":  "The part of you that wants to talk it through is anchored in meaning. The bond keeps pulling toward articulating the why of love — long-form, philosophical, in-depth. Write the longer piece; the version of you that explains love in depth is the version most worth listening to.",
    "Mercury-10": "The part of you that wants to talk it through is anchored in the public face of the bond. The words you say publicly become part of the relationship. Take care with the writing; what you say plainly today gets repeated by people who were not in the room.",
    "Mercury-11": "The part of you that wants to talk it through is anchored in the wider circle. Articulation here is for the network — messages, introductions, the threads that keep love-connections alive. Send the message you have been meaning to send; words are the maintenance work of networks.",
